title: The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 1 – The Flight Begins
description: The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 1 of 2: Michal lets David down through the window (Psalm 59), David feigns madness at Gath (Psalms 56 and 34), and Doeg the Edomite (Psalm 52), in the order of 1 Samuel, from the Psalm studies in the King James Bible.
output: lessons/bible/davids-flight-1-the-flight-begins.html

# The Psalms of David's Flight, part 1 of 2 (a Wednesday-night lesson, read from a laptop, about 30-35 minutes).
# Slide text is the author's own sentences, selected and set in the order of the 1 Samuel narrative, from:
#   Psalm 59 study (review/refile.json drive "psalms-59"), Psalm 56 study ("psalms-56", which also covers Psalm 34),
#   Psalm 34 series study (studies/data/books/Psalms.json, Psalms 34 main: 17833), Doeg study ("psalms-52-doeg"),
#   Psalm 52 series study (Psalms.json, Psalms 52 main: 18025, 18026). Verses typed in the studies are shown from the KJV.
# Layout (this series): title slide (overline "The Psalms of David's Flight · Part N of 2", heading, sub, refs), then
# "Where we are" with a step: line of the five episodes, the current part in bold. Each episode opens with a narrative
# slide (kicker "1 Samuel N · Place", the key verses as scripture:, the rest of the passage as [[ref]] buttons, a small
# note: with a rough minute mark), then 2-5 slides on the psalm: kicker "Psalm N · Place" -> heading -> one key verse
# from the psalm as big: -> at most two paragraphs -> an optional take: line. The part ends on "What we have seen".
# Fixed: nothing in the sentences shown needed a fix.
# The Psalm 34 heading "A Psalm of David, when he changed his behaviour before Abimelech; who drove him away, and he
# departed." is quoted by the study and is kept in its words (the KJV title of the psalm, not a numbered verse).

=== Introduction
--- The Flight Begins [title]
overline: The Psalms of David's Flight · Part 1 of 2
heading: The Flight Begins
sub: We often forget the flights of David were the impetus of so many Psalms.
refs: 1 Samuel 19:12; Psalm 59:16; Psalm 56:3; Psalm 34:4; Psalm 52:8
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- Where we are
kicker: The Psalms of David's Flight
heading: Part 1: The flight begins
tile: Psalm 59 | **The window** · 1 Samuel 19
tile: Psalms 56, 34 | **Gath** · 1 Samuel 21
tile: Psalm 52 | **Nob: Doeg** · 1 Samuel 21–22
tile: Psalm 54 | Ziph · 1 Samuel 23
tile: Psalm 57 | The cave at Engedi · 1 Samuel 24
p: A harrowing time, likely more than a decade where the future King lives as a fugitive, hunted, lied about and sought by wicked men in King Saul's service.

=== The window · Psalm 59
--- Michal let David down through a window
kicker: 1 Samuel 19 · The window
scripture: 1 Samuel 19:11-12 | mark: So Michal let David down through a window
p: Psalm 59 was written as Saul, David's King and father in law sent men to David's house to capture and kill him, while Michal, David's wife and daughter of the King aided in his escape, being lowered from the window to flee. [[1 Samuel 19:10]] [[1 Samuel 19:13-18]]
p: And we see that David's faithfulness towards God as His deliverer was present from the beginning.
note: About 3 minutes in

--- Without my fault
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
big: Psalm 59:4 | mark: without my fault
p: He trusts God to deliver him from the bloody men coming round his home, barking like dogs, saying cruel lies. [[Psalm 59:6-7]] David is innocent of wrongdoing, yet they seek his life. Without fault, pursued unto death, he cries out for the Lord to awake and help him.
p: This is similar to the prayer of Jesus, the sinless Son of God, in the hours leading up to His arrest. [[Matthew 26:39]] He, like David, could have asked for deliverance, and a host of angels to come and save Him. [[Matthew 26:53]] But for the sake of the cross, Jesus allowed them to take Him. [[Philippians 2:8]]
take: However, the willingness of Christ to be obedient unto death doesn't change the fact of Almighty God being able to deliver in a time of persecution.

--- The God of my mercy shall prevent me
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
big: Psalm 59:10 | mark: The God of my mercy shall prevent me
p: When David writes, *The God of my mercy shall prevent me*, he isn't using the modern understanding of the word, to stop or hinder, rather the actual definition of the word; to "go before" or "anticipate." His faith is that the mercy of God would be there with him, providing that means of escape. Knowing that the Lord would not leave him ashamed.
p: The mercy of God has already mapped out the boundary of the trial, and the window exit would be the means God had already allowed. [[1 Corinthians 10:13]]
take: And while Jesus rejected the hosts to save Him, David took the window, the way God made for his escape.

--- Thy mercy in the morning
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
big: Psalm 59:16 | mark: I will sing aloud of thy mercy in the morning
p: And, the night past, the fleeing David sings of the mercy of God in the morning, even as the dogs are barking around his door.
p: Just as David found deliverance in the morning, Christ’s voluntary obedience unto death led to the ultimate morning of the resurrection. Because He did not take the window, He broke the power of death entirely, ensuring that for every believer facing tribulation, hope maketh not ashamed. [[Romans 5:5]]
take: But God uses all things for good.

=== Gath · Psalms 56 and 34
--- He feigned himself mad
kicker: 1 Samuel 21 · Gath
scripture: 1 Samuel 21:12-13 | mark: was sore afraid of Achish; feigned himself mad
p: David has been delivered to the Kingdom of Gath by his flight. He stood at the gates, to seek an audience with King Achish. But, he notices the words of the guards, and the heightened senses sparked by fear cause him to sense potential trouble. [[1 Samuel 21:10-11]]
p: In this case fear helped David to react quickly. Rather than being taken captive and potentially turned over to Saul, David's feigned madness allows him to escape and continue his flight. [[1 Samuel 21:14-15]] His short-term fear protected him in a harrowing situation.
note: About 11 minutes in

--- What time I am afraid
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
big: Psalm 56:3
p: Psalm 56 was written commemorating the flight of David from Saul, when it seemed all Israel was against him and sought David's life. He prays for mercy from the Lord, in the midst of this tribulation, reminding himself that, when he is afraid, he will trust in God.
p: Being fearful is a sin. Having fear itself is not. We see David throughout the Psalms express fear. He is a mighty man of valor, great in battle, a giant-killer, a man of great faith, and yet he feels fear.
take: For some of us, *What time I am afraid, I will trust in thee* could be a life-verse.

--- Fear and being fearful
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
big: Psalm 56:4
p: Fear is a natural response to the threats against us and those that we love. Fear is something God has given us in order to react to immediate stimuli and avoid danger. Fear saves lives.
p: But being fearful, the condition where we take that warning system, and become slaves to it, where we allow present troubles of the world and being careful for everything, leave us unable to move, or to see the power of God in our lives. Our God is bigger than all the cares and tribulations of this life. [[Isaiah 41:10]]
take: The righteous man isn't fearless, he fears less.

--- Put thou my tears into thy bottle
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
big: Psalm 56:8
p: God loves His children, and knows our pain and struggles. He savors our prayer and captures our tears and numbers every step that we take, that He may direct our paths. Jesus says the same, speaking directly to us. [[Luke 12:7]]
p: The Lord knows our troubles and hears us in adversity. We have a Savior, Who the Bible says, understands what we go through. [[Hebrews 4:15]] He will never leave us or forsake us. We who praise His name and lean on His mercy, will never be ashamed.

--- I sought the LORD, and he heard me
kicker: Psalm 34 · Gath
big: Psalm 34:4
p: David's confidence at the time of fear, fleeing from Saul and finding himself being challenged at the gates of Gath led to his deliverance. Psalm 34 tells of this. It is a complement to Psalm 56. The Bible says it is, *A Psalm of David, when he changed his behaviour before Abimelech; who drove him away, and he departed.*
p: David credits the Lord with hearing and answering his cry. And for this, David gives joyful thanksgiving.
take: The confidence of [[Psalm 56:13]] ends in the song of worship, praising His name in Psalm 34.

--- O taste and see
kicker: Psalm 34 · Gath
big: Psalm 34:8
p: Give thanks in the congregation when God has moved in your life. Praise His holy name among the people. Teach others to fear the Lord. Tell them of the works He has done in your life.
p: While we are promised a longer life with more blessings, we are not promised a carefree life that has no suffering. Often the suffering we experience is for our benefit to help us grow in the Lord, and to increase our faith. [[Psalm 34:19]]
take: Our fear leads to faith, by casting our cares upon the Lord. Our faith leads to proclaiming the truth of Christ, teaching men to fear only God.

=== Nob · Psalm 52
--- Doeg the Edomite
kicker: 1 Samuel 21–22 · Nob
scripture: 1 Samuel 21:7 | mark: Doeg, an Edomite
p: David seeks food and weapons at the Tabernacle in Nob, from the unsuspecting priest, Ahimelech. But the priest isn’t the only person there to see David. Doeg is not a man of war. He is a herdsman. But he took the opportunity of seeing David there in Nob, on the run, to curry favor with Saul. [[1 Samuel 22:9-10]]
p: Saul refuses clemency. In his murderous rage he orders the death of the priest and entire lineage. [[1 Samuel 22:16-17]] Even Saul’s loyal men hesitate and refuse the order of their King, out of fear of the Lord and harming His anointed priests. But not Doeg the Edomite. [[1 Samuel 22:18-19]]
note: About 22 minutes in

--- O mighty man
kicker: Psalm 52 · Nob
big: Psalm 52:1 | mark: O mighty man
p: Doeg the shepherd, Doeg the herdsman, who brought out of date intelligence to Saul’s attention, jeopardizing the priest, that led to this situation, took full advantage of the license given him by Saul, and wickedly carried out the command. First slaying 85 priests, and then murdering every man, woman, and child, beast and all, found in the Levite city of Nob.
p: But Doeg became a byword. Psalm 52 mockingly called Doeg a "mighty man," for slaying men of God, dressed only in linen, and helpless women and children, even babies.

--- Root thee out of the land of the living
kicker: Psalm 52 · Nob
big: Psalm 52:5
p: We can imagine Doeg’s fate, the Bible doesn’t speak specifically of what became of him afterwards, but Psalm 52 gives us some idea of his ultimate fate before God.
p: If "selah" is meant to be a pause, a moment to reflect on what was just sung or said, is there anywhere more fitting than in that moment, having heard that sobering truth? And the righteous shall see their end and fear God, and laugh at the calamity they brought about on themselves. [[Psalm 52:6-7]] [[Proverbs 26:27]]

--- Like a green olive tree
kicker: Psalm 52 · Nob
big: Psalm 52:8
p: As the sole survivor of the high priestly line of Eli, Abiathar brings the official religious authority of Israel to David’s camp, signaling that God has abandoned Saul. [[1 Samuel 22:20-23]]
p: The evil of Saul and the bloodthirst of Doeg, became good for David, who loved God and was called according to His purpose. David trusted in the mercy of the Lord, and was strengthened by Doeg’s actions, and was able to sing of the praises of the Lord because of it.
take: But we are His. We have humbled ourselves to rely on His holy name.

=== What we have seen
--- What we have seen
point: The window | The mercy of God has already mapped out the boundary of the trial, and the window exit would be the means God had already allowed.
point: Gath | Fear is an opportunity to increase your faith and bring your feet to stay in His ways, to walk His paths.
point: Psalm 34 | Our fear leads to faith, by casting our cares upon the Lord.
point: Doeg | The evil of Saul and the bloodthirst of Doeg, became good for David.

--- Next week
kicker: Next week · Part 2 of 2
heading: The hunted king
step: 1 Samuel 23 · Psalm 54 | Ziph: the Ziphites
step: 1 Samuel 24 · Psalm 57 | Engedi: David spares Saul in the cave
p: David continues in tribulation for years, maintaining this outlook, as witnessed across the Psalm, from this initial trial, through the time in the wilderness, until God rewarded him with his crown. A man after God's own heart.
